    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d773">

                <pb facs="0813" n="801" sameAs="frus:frus1945v08/pg801"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867N.01/10–2845: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Syria</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Porter</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Damascus</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-10-28T16:00:00+02:00">October 28, 1945—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 29—10:05 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>76. At Foreign Office this morning where I called at Prime Minister’s request,
                    latter stated his Govt is much embarrassed at premature publication in Iraqi
                    press on October 26 of texts of Roosevelt–Kuwatly letters.<anchor n="95"
                        xml:id="d773-f1"/> He said that after my suggestion on October 24 that
                    publication be held up pending receipt Dept’s views, he had telegraphed Syrian
                    Missions abroad instructing no publication until further notice. He deeply
                    regrets this slip and hopes Dept will accept his apologies. With reference
                    Dept’s 72, October 26, decoded after this interview, he gave me to understand
                    that Syrians desired <pb facs="0814" n="802" xml:id="pg802"/> publication merely
                    as proof they, like Saud<anchor n="96" xml:id="d773-f2"/> and Abdullah,<anchor
                        n="97" xml:id="d773-f3"/> had not failed to bring their views on Palestine
                    to our attention.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Porter</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d773-f1" n="95" xml:id="d773-f1-content">
                <p>President Kuwatley’s letter of March 11 not printed; see footnote 39, <ref
                        target="#pg692">p. 692</ref>. For President Roosevelt’s letter of April 12,
                    see <ref target="#pg703">p. 703</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d773-f2" n="96" xml:id="d773-f2-content">
                <p>King Ibn Saud of Saudi Arabia.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d773-f3" n="97" xml:id="d773-f3-content">
                <p>Amir Abdullah of Transjordan.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
